# `Size` is already a quantity, so `amount of size` says the same thing twice.
# The one reading that is fine is `amount of size <noun>`, where `size` modifies
# the noun that follows it: "size information", "size pairs", "size bloat".
# So the rule only fires when `size` ends the noun phrase, which it does when the
# next token is a preposition, conjunction, determiner, pronoun, verb, or the end
# of the sentence. `AUX` is in the list because the tagger reports the copula as
# `AUX` in some sentences and `VERB` in others.
expr follows [ADP, CCONJ, SCONJ, PRON, DET, VERB, AUX]

expr main <[(!an amount of size @follows), (!an amount of size[., ?])], (amount of size)>

let message "Use `size` on its own."
let description "Corrects the redundant `amount of size` to `size`."
let kind "Redundancy"
let becomes "size"
let strategy "MatchCase"

test "I don't have that much amount of size." "I don't have that much size."
test "Equal steps in scale do not add the same amount of size." "Equal steps in scale do not add the same size."
test "The log file reaches certain amount of size." "The log file reaches certain size."
test "So that it does not depend on the amount of size of the node?" "So that it does not depend on the size of the node?"
test "There is a minimum amount of size required for file system structures." "There is a minimum size required for file system structures."
test "Determining real amount of size used in directory with hard links." "Determining real size used in directory with hard links."
test "Reports the amount of size in bytes taken up by hardware morph textures." "Reports the size in bytes taken up by hardware morph textures."
test "It needs significant amount of size and randomness to make them hard to guess." "It needs significant size and randomness to make them hard to guess."
test "This would still limit the amount of size a single request could buffer to disk." "This would still limit the size a single request could buffer to disk."
test "I think it is related to the amount of size I allocate for the db and log." "I think it is related to the size I allocate for the db and log."
test "I need a function to count amount of size from column C and D automatically." "I need a function to count size from column C and D automatically."
test "This job will consume your storage quota in the amount of size of git-svn clone." "This job will consume your storage quota in the size of git-svn clone."
test "I tend to get big files for the amount of size I have available." "I tend to get big files for the size I have available."
test "Double the hardware resources typically means double the amount of size and performance." "Double the hardware resources typically means double the size and performance."
test "WebP can save incredible amount of size if you have many images in your app." "WebP can save incredible size if you have many images in your app."
test "It derives its name from someone constrained by a given amount of size of knapsack." "It derives its name from someone constrained by a given size of knapsack."
test "Adding -g added huge amount of size to the debug builds." "Adding -g added huge size to the debug builds."
test "I DON'T HAVE THAT MUCH AMOUNT OF SIZE." "I DON'T HAVE THAT MUCH SIZE."

# `Size` modifies the noun after it in all of these, so `amount of` is doing real
# work and the phrase is correct.

allows "It's impossible for this API to exist and not leak some amount of size information."
allows "How to calculate the unique amount of size pairs for a given range."
allows "Some amount of size bloat is also acceptable."
allows "There was a significant amount of size difference after loading two dump files."
allows "We budgeted a fixed amount of size overhead per record."

allows "The amount of data written to disk stays small."
allows "That is a fair amount of work for one afternoon."
allows "The size of the index grew by four megabytes."

# Accepted gap: `an amount of size` is left alone. The fix there has to change the
# article as well, and the natural rewrite is `an amount`, not `a size`, so it is a
# different correction from the one this rule makes.

allows "I want to trade for an amount of size that is equivalent to 100 in cash."

# Second accepted gap, and it falls out of the first: the `!an` guard needs a token
# in front of `amount`, so a sentence that opens with `Amount of size ...` is not
# flagged. Every example in the report has the phrase mid-sentence.
